> ⚠️ **X9.63 KDF is deprecated and insecure in practice.**  
> It was standardized by ANSI X9.63 (2001), but it **lacks modern security properties**, such as resistance to attacks when the underlying hash function is weak. In particular, it uses **hash-based key derivation without salt, no iteration count, or proper domain separation**, making it vulnerable to brute-force and multi-target attacks.

> 🔐 **Recommended alternative:** Use **HKDF** (HMAC-based Key Derivation Function) with SHA-256/SHA-512 — defined in RFC 5869 — for all new applications. HKDF provides better security, flexibility, and clear design principles.

