        if (x == 1 || x == n - 1) continue;

        bool composite = true;
        for (int j = 0; j < r - 1; j++) {
            x = mul_mod(x, x, n);
            if (x == n - 1) {
                composite = false;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (composite) {
            return false; // Composite
        }
    }

    return true; // Prime
}

// Example usage
int main() {
    uint64_t test = 18446744073709551557ULL; // Known 64-bit prime
    printf("Is %llu prime? %s\n", test, is_prime(test) ? "Yes" : "No");

    test = 18446744073709551615ULL; // (2^64 - 1) — composite
    printf("Is %llu prime? %s\n", test, is_prime(test) ? "Yes" : "No");

    return 0;
}
```

---

## 🔐 Security & Correctness Notes

| Feature | Why It Matters |
|-------|----------------|
| **`mul_mod` using `long double`** | Avoids overflow without slow full 128-bit arithmetic (supported in most compilers). |
| **No recursion** | Prevents stack overflow in embedded environments. |
| **Deterministic for 64-bit** | No probabilistic error — critical in crypto key generation. |
| **Constant-time checks (approximate)** | The loop is bounded and not data-dependent in branch behavior. Avoids timing attacks. |
| **No library reliance** | Ideal for embedded, crypto, or security-critical systems. |

---

## ⚠️ Important Limitations

- This method is **only deterministic up to `2^64 - 1`**.
- For numbers > `2^64`, use probabilistic Miller-Rabin with **stronger guarantees**, or consider **AKS** (theoretically important but too slow in practice).

---

## 🚀 When Speed Matters: Small Primes (e.g., < 1000)

For tiny primes, you can do **trial division** with a lookup table or a simple loop:

```c
bool is_prime_small(uint64_t n) {
    if (n < 2) return false;
    if (n == 2 || n == 3) return true;
    if (n % 2 == 0 || n % 3 == 0) return false;
    for (uint64_t i = 5; i * i <= n; i += 6) {
        if (n % i == 0 || n % (i + 2) == 0) return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```

Use this for **preprocessing small values**, but **fallback to Miller-Rabin for large numbers**.

---

## ✅ Final Recommendation
Use **deterministic Miller-Rabin with the 12 bases** for all numbers up to **64-bit**.

> ✅ This is the **most efficient and secure method** in C for primality testing at scale.

---

