</div>
```

```css
/* Hide the native radio button */
.radio-hidden {
  appearance: none;
  -webkit-appearance: none;
  width: 0;
  height: 0;
  opacity: 0;
  position: absolute;
}

/* Style the custom radio button */
.radio-custom {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 20px;
  height: 20px;
  border: 2px solid #007bff;
  border-radius: 50%;
  margin-right: 8px;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background-color 0.2s;
}

/* Visual feedback when selected */
.radio-hidden:checked + .radio-custom {
  background-color: #007bff;
  border-color: #007bff;
}

/* Optional: Change color on hover */
.radio-custom:hover {
  border-color: #0056b3;
}

.radio-custom::after {
  content: '';
  display: block;
  width: 8px;
  height: 8px;
  margin: 6px;
  background-color: white;
  border-radius: 50%;
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 0.2s;
}

.radio-hidden:checked + .radio-custom::after {
  opacity: 1;
}
```

> ✅ **This gives full control over color, size, and appearance.**

---

#### 2. **Using `background-color` and `border-color`**
You can use CSS to modify the visual appearance of the default radio button (with limited success):

```css
input[type="radio"] {
  accent-color: #ff0000; /* This targets the native "accent" color (modern browsers) */
}
```

> ⚠️ **Note:** `accent-color` is supported in most modern browsers (Chrome 88+, Firefox 97+, Safari 15.4+), but it only affects the **fill color** of the native radio button — not full color control. It **does not** change the border or shape. It's **not a complete solution** for custom styling.

---

### 🔍 Security Researcher Perspective

- **UI Spoofing Risk**: Although not exploitable by itself, **misleading UIs** (e.g., hiding a truly malicious radio option behind a “green” label) could be used in **social engineering attacks**.
- **Accessibility**: Custom radio buttons **must maintain keyboard navigation, screen reader support, and ARIA attributes** to avoid accessibility issues — which can lead to compliance risks (e.g., WCAG violations).
- **Input Validation**: Changing appearance **does not bypass server-side validation**, but developers must ensure that **only valid values** are accepted — styling cannot be relied upon for security.
- **CSS Injection**: If user-controlled input is used in styling (e.g., via `style` attributes or `background-image: url(...)`) without sanitization, it could lead to **CSS injection** or **XSS** in rare cases.

> 🔐 **Best practice**: Always validate and sanitize any user input, even for visual properties.

